A||||171||drednot||who wears their seatbelts?||||6||
Z||000000||drednot||06-14-2001||07:04 PM||||I never have.  It's just a nasty habit.  When I had my Fury, It did not matter.  It was a K-frame HD car.  It weighed over 5000 pounds, and I'm sure that it would take out most trees/ other cars.  Now the Fiero...  I wonder if I could get an air bag put in.  My seats don't have "the Fiero Tear" in them because of it.||||reg||6
Z||000001||Sevaan||06-14-2001||07:08 PM||||Not wearing seatbelts is stupid.<P>What are you, worried you'll "wrinkle" your clothes? Get real. If it is an added layer of safety, then I say use it.<P>People who don't wear them are asking for it.<p>[This message has been edited by Sevaan (edited 06-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||SteveJ||06-14-2001||07:20 PM||||My family and I were saved by seat belts when the family Caravan was hit in the side and rolled over 360 degrees. We were not given advanced warning of this and would not have had time to put 'em on.||||reg||1
Z||000003||WampusCat||06-14-2001||07:21 PM||||I never leave the driveway without being strapped in.  I got into the habit from doing a lot of off roading as a teenager.  I needed the seatbelt to keep my hands and feet in contact with the wheel and pedals through the rough stuff. It's hard to drive when your head's banging off the roof.  Those old scare/driver-education flicks like "Room to Live" and "Signal 30" were influential too.  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||84Bill||06-14-2001||07:28 PM||||I saw a guy die in the stupidest little accident.<BR>He skidded on a wet road and did a 180, now sliding backwards and sideways (narrowly missing me) to the road he hit a really small curb about 6 inches high on the outside of the median divider right next to me. The car hopped up a foot or so and came back down, I SAW the poor soul driving hit his head on the roof of the car as it came back down, he was going up as the car came down. When he went down he never got back up. I jumped out real quick to see if he was ok and I could tell he was not. His body was slumped sideways over the center console and his head was bent over at a not so normal looking angle. The medics arrived and were working on him for a while before they took him away. I later read in the paper he did not make it.<BR>Funny thing I rememberd is the car was perfect except for the curb scuff on the rim. Not even a dent where it backed into the small tree.<BR>A lap belt would have saved him and his family||||reg||1
Z||000005||PeaceDawg||06-14-2001||07:30 PM||||I put my seatbelt on every time I drive a vehicle.  Good habit to develop.  Half the time I don't even realise that I am doing it...I just do.  I drive for a courier company and they write you up if you are seen not wearing one.<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000006||moonie||06-14-2001||07:41 PM||||i wear my seatbelt everytime i get in a car.<BR>in tennessee the coppers can pull you over i think just for not havine it on. i may be wrong , but i think its right.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||AkursedX||06-14-2001||07:54 PM||||I always wear my seatbelt, and I always make everyone who is driving with me buckle up.  It's just become a second nature thing for me.  But personally I wish I had a 4 or 5 point racing harness.  I would love to have one of those to strap me in the seat!||||reg||14
Z||000008||Songman||06-14-2001||08:28 PM||||Why does this keep coming up? People get killed for wearing seat belts just like they do for not wearing them... Only that the people who get killed for wearing them don't have a lobby working in Washington to make sure that money is spent on seatbelt research and improvement. <P>And it don't really matter who thinks it is stupid or how many figures you can pull up. It still ain't nobody's business but mine and any state that has a law about seat belts is not reading the Constitution very well. We went through the same thing with helmets for motorcycles and now they are being overturned all over the country.<P>And as I have said REPEATEDLY when this comes up.. what next? Some group or individual that wants a little attention does a research project that finds that too many people are dying from head injuries so they start bucking for a law for helmets on all people in a car... Then flame suits when the realize that cars can catch on fire... There are just some things in life that there should not be a law about...<P>And what is even more amazing to me is how it always gets personal if you don't follow the crowd. As stated above, "Not wearing seatbelts is stupid." I don't wear seatbelts.. never have and never will. I'll match IQs with anyone in here and we'll see if it is about stupidity or choice. Maybe it is just me but I think people who 1) think everyone has to do things just they way they do it, and 2) have to resort to name calling and insults to prove their point... those are the ones that need to have their intelligence questioned...<P>Notice how I did all of that and didn't call anyone a name... heheh<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Bluebottlejunky||06-14-2001||08:36 PM||||I do, I mean its worth it....if it will save yor life.. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  I see all my freinds driving like a bad otta hell when thy drive..and with no seatbelt on..at least in Wisconsin you get pulled over for not wearing one..$50 fine! <P>------------------<BR>Hello, My name is Mike and I am addicted too fiero's...PLEASE dont help me.<BR>85'GT 4sp<BR>AIM: Fierofanatic2001||||reg||1
Z||000010||Justin||06-14-2001||08:39 PM||||My stepmom was in an accident and she wasn't wearing her seatbelt.  She was thrown from the car, and the car contiued on it's crash route.  If she would have had her seatbelt on, she would have been killed.  The whole drivers side area was crushed flat.  She was able to go home from the hospital the next day with a few scrapes and bruises.  Hows that for "it's stupid not to wear your seatbelt"?  I think laws against not wearing your seatbelt are ridiculous.  Your not harming anyone else by not wearing your seatbelt.  That's like saying it's illegal to commite suicide.  I wear my seatbelt every time I drive, simply because if your in an accident, chances are a seatbelt will do more good then harm.  I like the odds of that.  But I don't put people down for not wearing it.  It's there choice.<P>------------------<BR>If at first you don't succeed, pretend you were never trying in the first place.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.plauder-smilies.de/rough/greenchainsaw.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Wolfhound||06-14-2001||08:44 PM||||Here's a neat little sign coming into town.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Et.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Predator||06-14-2001||08:48 PM||||I went off a 50 foot bank about 16 years ago in a GTX. I had my seatbelt on. My head would not have still been attached to my shoulders if I was not wearing it. The car landed on its roof crushing it, and the seat forward. It was so close they had to cut my hair to get me out, and I don't have long hair. The hair was caught in between the crushed windshield and the dash. If I had not had my seatbelt on I would be dead. That is the only statistic I need. I do agree that there should not be a law making you wear a seatbelt. That is your choice. I do on the other hand aggree that children should wear a seat belt and be in a car seat and child restraint of some kind. They are not old enough to make a decision to wear one or not. This is all I am going to say on this subject. It is another can of worms being opened, and is a matter of opinion, and is just going to start another flame war here. I am not going to talk you into wearing a seatbelt, and you are not going to talk me out of wearing one. <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero4_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||Songman||06-14-2001||08:55 PM||||Agreed Predator... everyone has the same stories and statistics as the last few times this came up. And as you said, children are not old enough to make a decision and should be buckled in. Also, they don't reflexes like an adult to help them in an accident situation...<P>I told the story before of my friends whose car was cut in half horizontally by a semi out of control. Had they had belts they could not have dove into the floorboard and their top halfs would have been gone with the roof and the seatbacks... And another friend was thrown through his open T-tops and out of his Trans Am when he hit a telephone pole. The motor came into his seat. No chance had he been there.<P>As I said, there are stories and statistics for both sides. Everybody just do what they feel comfortable with as long as you aren't hurting anyone else... Hey! That's a pretty good rule for every situation!<P>I'm out...<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000014||fierohoho||06-14-2001||09:22 PM||||I have had two vehicles totaled while I was the driver. <BR>These were front end collisions. <P>The first one was in my 1972 Cougar, I hit a tree at about 40mph, because of my seatbelt I walked away with a scratch on my left elbow. <P>The second was my 1984 Yugo, (give me s*** about that in O/T) I broadsided a 4dr Chev Impala at about 30mph, I walked away from this one without a scratch. Both times I was wearing a seat and shoulder restraint.<BR>I speak from experience that they work.<P>I have also pulled people out of wrecks who haven't been wearing theirs who would have more often than not been treated and released, but instead I watch as they get air lifted to the nearest trauma center.<P>I respect anyones right to not wear their seatbelts, but in the long run it's not them that pay the price it's their friends and family who do.<P>I have heard the excuses, I don't want to be trapped in my car by the seatbelt if it "catches fire" or "goes into a lake", my arguement for that is, If you have your belt on you have a better chance to escape the car in those circumstances as your injuries will statistically be less severe. <P>I tell my friends that I wear mine so if there is an accident I can get go get them some help because they didn't wear theirs. <P>Sorry for the ramble, I hate dead people, they're not much fun to pickup and move.<P>I'll put a "give me grief about having owned a Yugo" in O/T<P>------------------<BR>'84 SE 2.5l<BR>'85 Coupe 2.5l now getting a 3800<BR>'86 SE 2.5l||||reg||1
Z||000015||LZeitgeist||06-14-2001||09:29 PM||||The "Fiero Tear" doesn't happen from the seatbelt - it happens from the driver sliding their ass across the outboard bolster every time they get in and out of the car. <P>Wear it or not - just like Darwin wrote, it's survival of the fittest. But if you ride in *my* car, you wear your seatbelt, or you get the F out. If the sh*t hits the fan, I'm not going to have your death on *my* conscious.<P>The reason I feel that way? I owe my mother's life to a seatbelt. <P>My parents/family never used seatbelts to my recollection. But when I took Driver's Ed in high school, they drilled into you to wear your seatbelt. You wouldn't pass the course unless you wore your seatbelt. It became second nature to me - get in, shut the door, reach for the belt, buckle in, start the car, release the parking brake, drive off.<P>After I got my license, my Mom used to ride with me when she wanted to go on errands. It gave me a chance to drive, and it gave her a chance to get her errands done without having to fight the traffic. Since I was taught by Driver's Ed to wear my seatbelt, I told her that I wasn't going to move the car until she was buckled in. She agreed, and got into the habit herself. <P>Two years later, after wearing her seatbelt became second nature to her, she was in an accident - a car that was waiting to turn left across her lane (waiting for her to go past) was rear-ended by a van, and since the car was waiting with its front wheels already turned (stupid), it was pushed into her lane when she was mere feet away from it. Due to the seatbelt, she suffered a cracked breastbone, and some ugly bruises where the seatbelt arrested her forward motion. The car was totaled - and if she'd not been wearing her seatbelt, she'd have either been smashed onto the steering column and dash, or if she was lucky, she'd have gone through the windshield. IN THAT PARTICULAR CASE, the seat belt saved her life. <P>It is true that the seat belt is not a perfect thing - there are as many people bitching about 'being trapped in the car' as there are people proclaiming that they lived because they stayed in the driver's seat. I *do* feel it should be up to personal choice. (It seems that the Government's role in today's society is to save society from Darwin's influence. No wonder society is going to Hell in a handbasket - nothing left to cull the weak and the stupid.) I *don't* like the laws that are in place to protect me from my own stupidity. However, I can only relate the experience I know - and that is the experience of still having a Mother for the past 12 years because of a strap of nylon webbing and a working ratchet assembly. <P>Do as you will. I know what I'm going to be doing.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by LZeitgeist (edited 06-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||hyhy4u||06-14-2001||09:43 PM||||I personally always put on my seatbelt and make everyone else in the car to...i understand that sometimes not wearing your seatbelt can save you but you got a lot better chance of it doing more good then harm.||||reg||1
Z||000017||btoth||06-14-2001||09:48 PM||||I wear my seatbelt when I drive mostly to hold myself in the seat when I take turns or stop too fast.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  They do stop you from going under the dash or out the window (well, maybe not in a Fiero) if you rear-end someone (which I've done) or get hit from behind (had that happen too).  Other than that... I don't know.  Some car's seat belts scare me.  The belts in my friend's Grand Prix GTP grab me right around my stomach and I'm sure that if we did hit someone I'd be torn in two.  That would suck.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I've got in the habit of always wearing it, but I do think that it's a pointless law.||||reg||1
Z||000018||LZeitgeist||06-14-2001||09:52 PM||||Another note - <P>Put a raw egg in a coffee can, and roll it down a hill. <P>Then take another raw egg, and another coffee can, and tape the egg to the inside of the coffee can. Now roll *this* can down the same hill.<P>See which egg survives. <P>The seat belt is there to hold you in place while the car is being destroyed around you. The destruction usually takes an instant - and if the cargo (meaning you) in the car stays where the automotive designers calculated it would during the destruction, the cargo will have the maximum amount of protection that was designed into the car. Otherwise, you're a frickin' raw egg in a coffee can. <P>Splut. <P><BR>Survival of the fittest, baby.||||reg||1
Z||000019||Cliff Pennock||06-14-2001||10:08 PM||||I <B>always</B> wear a seatbelt and it has saved my life before. I also always insist on my passenger wearing a seatbelt. When I'm behind the wheel the passenger is my responsibility. Don't want to wear a seatbelt? Then get out of my car.<P>If you wear <B>your</B> seatbelt is totally up to you. Statistics have shown over and over again that wearing a seatbelt has saved much more lives than <B>not</B> wearing a seatbelt. It's like 10,000 to 1. There's no question about that and there should be no discussion about that too.<P>Again, if you don't want to wear your seatbelt than that's up to you. But don't use arguments like "I know someone who survived an accident because he <B>wasn't</B> wearing his seatbelt - therefore I don't wear seatbelts". Because for every such story, there are 10,000 stories to the contrary.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000020||fyerstarter||06-14-2001||10:08 PM||||I also think it is rediculous to have a law telling you to wear a seatbelt, or that you can't ride in the back of a pickup truck, or to wear a motorcycle helmet, or not to do drugs, or smoke cigarettes, and so on. But face it, that is the way America works. People say this is a free country, far from it. It is a free country if you are part of the majority, if you are a white middle class suburbanite who does nothing but sleep, eat, and watch TV then you are free as a bird to do those things......for now.<P>But if you want to take your jetski or your dirtbike out and play on the weekends you cant because two strokes are fast becoming illegal. <P>If you want to supe up your car for a little extra performance you can't because of emmisions laws.<P>If you want to get high and relax with friends in your own home you can't because of drug laws.<P>If you want to carry a handgun on your person or in your car to protect yourself and your family you can't because of gun laws. <P>All of these things are activities enjoyed by the minority and thus are not of any concern to your average American. <P>My definition of freedom is having the ability to do whatever YOU want, as long as you don't hurt anyone else in the process. Being able to do whatever the government tells you is not freedom. <P><BR>I always wear my seatbelt by choice.||||reg||1
Z||000021||DRH||06-14-2001||10:09 PM||||I usually wear mine now. I never did when I was younger (I've owned cars that never even had seat belts). I started wearing them on the Interstate before it was the law. I wear them most of the time now because it's the law. It gripes me a little because I think it should be my choice... but whatever, it really doesn't get in the way and I don't have to fool with putting it on later if I get to driving fast. <P>I know there are stories of people being better without them in certain situations... I still think the odds are in favor of being better off with them in most accidents though. I figure I got a better chance of getting out of a burning or sinking car if my head didn't just slam into the steering wheel at 60 MPH.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Now motorcycle helmets are a different story. WI doesn't require them and I haven't worn one in years. Everywhere else I've lived did require them and I (almost) always wore one. It's a whole different experience with or without a helmet. And yes, I know it's more dangerous riding without a helmet. If I wanted to be safe I wouldn't be on the thing in the first place!!! It should be my choice.<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000022||coolcat||06-14-2001||10:11 PM||||This is whats left of my car. If my friend haden't been wearing there seat belt they would have been thrown through the windshield. So, yes I wear mind and if you want to rind in my car you have to wear it...   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/scan2_5.JPG">   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/scan3_4.JPG"> <BR>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/headbang.gif">   Gettin good players is easy,gettin them to play together is the hard part<P>[This message has been edited by coolcat (edited 06-14-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by coolcat (edited 06-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000023||Ken Wittlief||06-14-2001||10:23 PM||||Who wears their seat belt?<P>all race car drivers<P>all aircraft pilots<P>all coast guard search and rescue crewmen are strapped in in case their boat capsizes (most of the boats are self righting).<P> I only wish I could put 5 point seat belts in my car, and I wish you could buy new cars with 5 point seat belts INSTEAD of airbags.<P>   airbags are only useful if occupants are NOT wearing their seatbelts. So all you boneheads out there that refuse to wear yours, you are the reason that all new cars are required to have TWO airbags in them now - which drives the cost of the car up around $2,500.<P>   so figure out how much money you are costing the rest of us!<P>  If you dont want to wear your seatbelt thats fine with me on one level, as long as you refuse treatment at any hospital that receives any kinda tax dollars or government funding, and as long as you pay all your medical expenses out of your own pocket, and as long as you get ZERO dollars from your insurance company if you die  - because all that money comes out of MY POCKET!<P>  You wanna be reckless and careless - fine - but do it at your own expense - and dont expect us to put your orphaned children through college either.<P>   But there is more to it than that. When you are strapped in you stay in the drivers seat. If you are not strapped in you can be knocked out of the drivers seat and be unable to reach the brakes or steering wheel, or you can be knocked unconscience, then your car is completely out of control. THAT is why its the law in most states - your car will do far more damage if you are unable to apply the brakes or steer it at all.<P>   the argument about the woman being thrown from the car is invalid for this same reason. If she were still at the wheel, the car may not have ended up crushed the same way. She may have walked away with no injuries.<P>   I know several accounts of accidents, but I will only tell you one. A chemistry teacher at my high school (Hutch tech in buffalo) was also a voulenteer fireman. He got a call one night, a 69 mustang nailed a bridge support. <P>  when they got there the driver was semiconscious, and pretty messed up. after several minutes they realized he was asking how his girlfriend was. There was nobody else in the car, they checked twice.<P>   just as they were about to take him away in the ambulance he started yelling , she was in the car, she was in the car, where is she, where is she?<P>   they though maybe she was thrown out and looked around. Then my teacher took one last look in the car. He looked up under the dashboard and saw what looked like a rag stuffed up where the glove box should be. <P>   what he saw was her thigh, about from her knee and about a foot up. thats all that was visable.<P>   she had been completely stuffed up under the dashboard by the force of the impact. And she was dead. If the driver had not told them, they would never even have known she was in the car.<P>  not wear your seatbelt?! you gotta be nuts!||||reg||1
Z||000024||Jaygee79||06-14-2001||10:40 PM||||I always wear my seatbelt.  I don't feel safe without it.  It's true that it is possible to be hurt by one but it FAR more likely that it will save you.  Unlike airbags, I must say.  Now those things should be banned.<P>------------------<BR>Jaime<BR>84SE red<BR>AOL IM: Jaygee79<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/jaygee79/index.html" TARGET=_blank>Jaygee79's Web Page</A><BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pooheey1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000025||danny boy||06-14-2001||10:42 PM|||| There is a law in the books that states workers shall not be physically confined by means of chains,straps,belts to their woekplace. Aruuably that means it is illegal for commercial drivers to wear seatbelts. <BR>Do I? Religiously.||||reg||1
Z||000026||Galen||06-14-2001||10:45 PM||||I guess i'm nutts.. I've been in accidents.. never have I had a seatbelt on..I hit the back bumper of a dumba&& lady who pulled outin front of my 88.. no real big damage so that one wont be entered into the evidence. <P>My dad ran a greenlight(long story) and hit a celebrity in my moms 94 camaro, I was in the backseat and got slammed into the back of the front seat. If I had been wearing my seatbelt, I would have gotten slammed into the seat belt. I prefer a black eye to bruises throught my chest and abdomen regions.<P>My brother had another dumba&& woman pull out in fornt of his 76 Ford f100. Slammed into her at 55. The truck got slung around and we came to a stop about 15 feet later. Very painful, no seat belt. I hit the dash with my knee and something with my chin, not real sure. Didn't know we had hit something till I was looking back the way we came(we were slung around) and saw the Taures crumpled in the side. Would the seat belt have helped me there? I highly doubt it. Okie my head would have prolly hit the dash still, my knee wouldn't. I guess thats the value of the shoulder harness. <P>Overall, I have to agree with songman thatit should be a personal choice. I personally do choose to wear it sometimes now, just becuz I hav gotten two 25$ tickets by stupid cops in the past year. Thats the only reason. If I want to run the higher risk of getting hurt in some cases, and less in others by not wearing my seat belt. I should be legally given that choice. for us americans, We live in the United States of the Offended & the followers. <P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/pictures/redcreature.gif">     <BR> <A HREF="mailto:galen@spinrpg.com">galen@spinrpg.com</A>  -<A HREF="http://myfiero.spinrpg.com/" TARGET=_blank>1988 Hawaiin Orchid Coupe w/ sunroof</A><BR>AIM: GalenRPG - <A HREF="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/" TARGET=_blank>WebJolt Pictures</A>||||reg||1
Z||000027||LZeitgeist||06-14-2001||10:47 PM||||OSHA also requires that workers be trained in the proper use of the safety equipment applicable to their jobs. <P>"Chained"? Hardly. "Safer"? You betcha. Studies have proven it. Time and time again.<P>Oh, and Ken - airbags are next to useless unless the seatbelts *are* worn - an airbag is designed to take an impact from a certain range of motion. If the occupant isn't prevented from leaving those degrees of movement, then the airbag won't help them. The seatbelts keep the occupant "in the safety envelope", so to speak.||||reg||1
Z||000028||Her86GT||06-14-2001||10:54 PM||||My brother was riding as a passenger in my other brothers car.  He thought to himself half way through their trip "maybe I should put on my seatbelt - just in case".  Just at that moment, my brother driving lost control and ran off the road.  My passenger brother didn't get his seatbelt on in time and went through the windshield!!!  It can happen to anyone at any time  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  Luckily he is still alive but is a very nervous driver who always puts his seatbelt on before he takes off.  <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mycar2sm.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by Her86GT (edited 06-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000029||DJRice||06-14-2001||10:56 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by LZeitgeist:<BR><B><P>Oh, and Ken - airbags are next to useless unless the seatbelts *are* worn - an airbag is designed to take an impact from a certain range of motion. If the occupant isn't prevented from leaving those degrees of movement, then the airbag won't help them. The seatbelts keep the occupant "in the safety envelope", so to speak.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I wouldnt go so far as to call airbags useless to those who are wearing seatbelts.  If we did not have airbags, EMTs would not be able to charge for washing that dust out of ones eyes....And just think of all of the doctors that would not get to treat broken arms that were knocked into the cardoor or window.  Airbags are very useful with seatbelts.  <P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/djr_signature.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000030||Formula88||06-14-2001||11:13 PM||||Ok, I wear my seatbelt because of safety, and to me it's more comfortable.  I don't like feeling like I can slide around the car.  Also, I think that it's my choice and no government should be making a law to tell me to wear a seatbelt.  Is it a good idea?  Sure.  Can not wearing one be dangerous?  True again.  But you know, sticking a screwdriver in your eye is bad.  We should have a law against it.  You can't legislate stupidity.  You can't protect people from themselves.  If I make the choice not to wear a seatbelt, that's my god given, Constitution protect right!<P>Now, airbags, IMO, are a whole other can of worms. Yes, they can save many lives, but when an advance in the airbag industry is to make them "low energy" so the safety device is less likely to KILL YOU, something is wrong!  They aren't any good if you're not wearing your seatbelt anyway.  You'll just slide under the bag, and it'll hit you in the face, not the chest.  It's more dangerous than no airbag and no seatbelt!<P>This country was founded on choice and personal freedom.  We need to remind our legislators of that fact come election time.||||reg||6
Z||000031||Formula88||06-14-2001||11:17 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by drednot:<BR><B>I never have.  It's just a nasty habit.  When I had my Fury, It did not matter.  It was a K-frame HD car.  It weighed over 5000 pounds, and I'm sure that it would take out most trees/ other cars.  Now the Fiero...  I wonder if I could get an air bag put in.  My seats don't have "the Fiero Tear" in them because of it.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That's your choice, but your logic is flawed.  The fact that your old Fury was a K-frame HD car weighing over 5000 pounds simply meant the CAR was more likely to survive, not you.  You're just as fragile not matter what you're in.  If the car survives, but you hit the windshield at 50mph, you're still screwed.  <P>Not a flame, just pointing out a misconception in physics.<P>||||reg||13
Z||000032||Ken Wittlief||06-14-2001||11:37 PM||||where is all this personal choice and constitutional rights BS coming from?!?!<P> you dont want to wear your seat belts? Fine. Stay on your private roads and highways. <P>  But when you are on OUR roads and highways, you follow the laws and regulations that we as a socity have agreed to.<P>  You put your headlites on at night. You drive at a reasonable speed. You keep your car under control. You keep your car in proper operating condition and have it inspected if necessary. You drive sober. And you and all your passengers wear your seatbelts.<P>   the motor vehicle transportation system that has been developed in this country is exactly that, a SYSTEM. it includes the rules by which cars are designed and produced and tested, the regulations by which they must be maintained, the training that all drivers are required to have, the safety features all cars must have, the snow plows, the highway maintance crews, the highway patrols, the rescue services and personnel, the hospitals and rehabilitation centers, the insurance companies, the power companies that keep the street lights on...<P>  you have NO constitutional right to drive a car in this transportation system. If you agree to the rules and regulations that govern it, you are granted the PRIVALEDGE to use it.<P>  If you dont want to follow all the rules, stay off the roads. Drive on your own private highways. And when you wreck your car, call a private ambulance to haul you to a private hospital.||||reg||1
Z||000033||84Bill||06-14-2001||11:44 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ken Wittlief:<BR><B>Who wears their seat belt?<P>all aircraft pilots <P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>AND EVERY SINGLE PASSANGER a by federal law an aircraft is not permitted to take off until all passangers and crew are seated and properly restrained<P>I buckel up 99% of the time there are rare occasions where I do not and I usually feel naked without it.<BR>When I get in my Fiero and strap in I allmost feel one with the car. I may sound strange but it's almost like I wear my car like a piece of clothing  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><P>Anywho I can say I have been in 2 major wrecks 1 without a belt 30 mph brick wall (don't ask) suffered no long tttt tterm efffffects but was hurting for 3 weeks. and one with a 45 MPH into another car end result was the obligatory belt bruise across my upper chest but no other pain. the impacts felt about the same from what I remember but I'd rather crash at 50 mph belted than 30 unbelted. jeazuse that 30 mph hurt me and totaled my 86 Fiero. fortunatly for me I had my arm up over my face in a defensive way and it prevented my face from becoming one with the windshield. I did cause a nice laceration on my elbow and wrist my knees ripped the instrument panel off its mounts and crushed it into the dash and broke into 100 pieces. I had a semi circular bruise around the upper portion of my chest. Wy feet had pushed the brake pedal so hard it bent the shaft to the MC and the clutch slammed the floor so hard it left a 1/2 dollar sized dent in the floor pan, the front end was dead flat to just a few inches shy of the rear of the headlight wells. both door jambs had impressions of the door stricker plate and the lower front portion of the winshield frame buckeled up and back about 1/2 inch. I can safely say jeasus was my copilot on that trip  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Since then I'm a bit older and a lot wiser.<P>Glad to see so many of us do wear our belts. That rocks!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000034||MsBeth||06-14-2001||11:50 PM||||Always wear one ... 10 years ago I was stopped in a line of traffic at the bottom of hill with a line of cars coming the other way and some dip comes speeding down the hill (25 speed limit) right into my backend- if I had not had my seatbelt on I would have gone right through the windshield- and if I hadn't cut the wheel all the way to the right I would have been a MsBeth filling in a very nasty car sandwich!  As it was my frame was so twisted the car was wrecked!  I was fine - just a pulled muscled - in my neck from the force of the impact. <P>And just before that had a boyfriend get drunk - hit a tree - went through the windshield and ended up in ICU with 400 stiches in his face- if he had had his belt on he would have been ok. (of course if he had not got in the car to begin with he would have been ok  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> )<P>MsBeth<p>[This message has been edited by MsBeth (edited 06-14-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000035||Galen||06-15-2001||12:10 AM||||Ken, get off your pedastool for a second and read whats been said. You'll see the light(cept in cali). So you are saying these arn't our highways as well? we dont pay taxes on the roads as well? Glad to know you're the only one paying for the roads. Its not fair to force some drivers & passengers to wear seatbelts and others not. my brother had a 57 ford, that didn't have seatbelts, wasn't a option on the car... is it fair to ticket that driver for no seatbelt? when the car didn't come with one?<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/pictures/redcreature.gif">     <BR> <A HREF="mailto:galen@spinrpg.com">galen@spinrpg.com</A>  -<A HREF="http://myfiero.spinrpg.com/" TARGET=_blank>1988 Hawaiin Orchid Coupe w/ sunroof</A><BR>AIM: GalenRPG - <A HREF="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/" TARGET=_blank>WebJolt Pictures</A>||||reg||1
Z||000036||Ken Wittlief||06-15-2001||12:38 AM||||everyone who pays taxes has partial ownership of all public property - to be used as we agree to as a society.<P>  go down and tell the mayor you want to sleep in his office at night from now on, cause you own it too.<P>  you are free to walk up and down the roads all you want. But if you want to operate a motor vehicle in public, you have to agree to follow the rules. Everybody. The same rules.<P>   to do otherwise would be chaos.<P>  to let each individual pick and choose which rules to follow and which ones to claim "constitutional rights" over  - would be insane! <P>  and believe it or not, it goes both ways. I would like to have a 5 point seat belt in my car, instead of the stock 3 point one. But in most states they are illegal, because they are harder to get out of in an emergency, and most passerbys that come to assist you in an accident would not know how to get you out of your car. So even though they are safer, they are not allowed.<P>  The system is not perfect. There are no guarentees. Based on years and years of crash test and accident investigations, and automotive research, we have what we have.<P>  If you follow the program you have the best odds of minimum injury in a wreck, and the best odds of causing the minimum damage to others life and property. <P>  I think it would be really cool to set the cruise control, climb up on the roll bar through the open sunroof, and steer with my feet while sitting on the roof at 65mph on the highway. <P>   Dont I have the right to?||||reg||1
Z||000037||Galen||06-15-2001||01:03 AM||||You have the right to act out of stupidity?<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/pictures/redcreature.gif">     <BR> <A HREF="mailto:galen@spinrpg.com">galen@spinrpg.com</A>  -<A HREF="http://myfiero.spinrpg.com/" TARGET=_blank>1988 Hawaiin Orchid Coupe w/ sunroof</A><BR>AIM: GalenRPG - <A HREF="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/" TARGET=_blank>WebJolt Pictures</A>||||reg||1
Z||000038||rodmcneill||06-15-2001||01:07 AM||||As long as your feet are strapped in, with at least sandles.||||reg||12
Z||000039||kslish||06-15-2001||01:12 AM||||I always wear my seatbelt.  I feel naked without it on when the car is in motion...  I think it's because of the time I hydroplaned into the rear of a Toyota Camry a few years ago.<P> <IMG SRC="http://homepage.mac.com/kjslish/Fiero/crash2.jpg"> <P>The seatbelt did it's job, the steering wheel and windshield would have hurt.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR>Ken S.<BR>'87GT and '86SC<BR>See them at <A HREF="http://homepage.mac.com/kjslish/Fiero.html" TARGET=_blank>http://homepage.mac.com/kjslish/Fiero.html</A><BR>Western PA Fiero Club - <A HREF="http://www.westpennfiero.org" TARGET=_blank>www.westpennfiero.org</A>||||reg||1
Z||000040||Songman||06-15-2001||01:33 AM||||   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ken Wittlief:<BR><B>where is all this personal choice and constitutional rights BS coming from?!?!<P> you dont want to wear your seat belts? Fine. Stay on your private roads and highways. <P>  But when you are on OUR roads and highways, you follow the laws and regulations that we as a socity have agreed to.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Oh please! I have listened to all this rubbish and kept quiet about as long as I can! When did they become YOUR damn roads? I'm 36 years old, been working since I was 15. Made over $100,000 a year for most of the last 15 and you think MY tax money doesn't give me the right to drive on YOUR roads.... this is why I try to stay away from this crap! People say anything that fits their own little rant and try to pretend that it is right. Trust me... anyone who believes this better wake up. There are a lot of people on your little private transit system who will disagree with you.<P>And I'm costing YOU money? What is all that money that I pay all of the time? I've paid insurance premiums for my entire working life and have used maybe 1% of it if that, but I am costing YOU money! Give me a break!<P>And thanks so much for proving my point about making it personal and name calling, 'bonehead'.. I think that is one of the names you used, Ken... Since I am costing you all of this money and you are paying for me, just go ahead and get my room ready. I'll go ahead and move in and start saving all this money I thought I was spending on paying for myself...<P>And I'm not going to respond to everything I disagree with that I have read tonight. This post would be too long... But I am going to say one more thing...I have paid several tickets too for not wearing my seatbelt. And I would pay them again if I had to. But the last time a cop said he was going to ticket me for not wearing a seatbelt I told him that he could write it but I wouldn't take it because it was an illegal law. He didn't write the ticket. And the motorcycle cop that gave me a ticket about a month ago, he never even thought about it even though he stood there for 15 minutes talking to me... Motorcycle riders, even cops, know how ridiculous it is...<P>Oh, I do have to say one more thing... What are you calling Constitutional Rights BS? I bet you voted for Gore, didn't you? He didn't have too much use for the Constitution either. Thank God we got rid of him.. I hope that island Alec Baldwin said he was gonna move to is big enough for all of you people who think YOUR way is the only way.. <P>I'm done with this thread... sheesh!<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A><p>[This message has been edited by Songman (edited 06-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000041||Jaygee79||06-15-2001||08:28 AM||||There was a good point that someone made in an earlier post (I forget who).  If you are not wearing your seatbelt and get hit, you can end up somewhere other than the driver's seat and unable to control the vehicle, thereby endangering others.  If you are in a seatbelt, you can stay there and (hopefully) get the car back into control, limiting the danger.  Just a point I wanted to make.<P>------------------<BR>Jaime<BR>84SE red<BR>AOL IM: Jaygee79<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/jaygee79/index.html" TARGET=_blank>Jaygee79's Web Page</A><BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pooheey1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000042||84Bill||06-15-2001||08:28 AM||||Songman<BR>Lets look ath the big picture here<P>Lets see you don't wear a belt, Fine your choice. You get into an accident, thats not a problem so the you go to the hospital and get fixed up. Here is where the problem starts, Had you been wearing a belt you would not need a hospital and this disertation would end here. But lets say you didnt wear one. Do you have medical insurance? you do? great! Please sign here and your insurance company will pay the 15,000 dollar bill. Well you see our medical premiums goes up because you and several million others were not wearing a seatbelt like yourself. I have a problem with this. I have a problem with insurance companies too. BUT I don't make the rules I just play by them. You break the rules and something happens we all pay for it. I mean it's just a stupid little piece of fabric, that would have saved 14,500 bucks. I'm not saying your not going to get hurt in an accident by wearing a seatbelt but for the vast majority of situations it just costs less if you have it on, besides is it really so hard to operate? Reach over and click, your done and after you do it a few hunderd times it becomes automatic and you don't even have to bother with the horrable task of consious effort. <P>Oh and lets say you didn't have medical insurance who pays the bill? Oh yeah thats right, we all have to pay. OK lets go one step further you have no insurance at all and end up in a hospital who pays then? UHHHH yeah we all do. Just because you don't feel like making it click or because it will wrinkel my cloths or I'll get trapped and die in a firey wreck or the car will roll over and crush me HA!! I love that one most of all. Whatever the lame excuse, you have a much better chance of avoiding serious injury if you wear it PERIOD<BR><p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 06-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000043||Denny||06-15-2001||08:31 AM||||There have been about 30 young people killed around the Lancaster area in the last 5 years, and at least that many people disabled, from being ejected from vehicles during accidents of varying degrees of violence.  Nearly all would be OK if they had been wearing seatbelts.<P>Nobody rides with me unless they're belted in.<P>Wear them!||||reg||4
Z||000044||Formula88||06-15-2001||09:49 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ken Wittlief:<BR><B>where is all this personal choice and constitutional rights BS coming from?!?!<BR>{snip}<BR>  But when you are on OUR roads and highways, you follow the laws and regulations that we as a socity have agreed to.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Correct.  I agree completely.  Which is why I encourage people to make sure their elected officials are passing the laws they agree with.  I personally would like to see the seat belt law in NC repealed.  I would wear mine anyway, but I don't like the kind of law that intrudes, IMO, on my personal freedom.  I've heard all the arguments for and against, but when it boils down to it, the seatbelt is for my safety. I should have the choice.  Keeping my car in good order, driving safely, etc.  These all affect other motorists directly, so I have no problem with that.  If Joe Blo drives past me w/o a seat belt on, am I in danger?  No.  If he drives past me drunk in an unsafe car at 80mph, am I in danger - I sure am.  And therein lies the difference.<BR>||||reg||3
Z||000045||Formula88||06-15-2001||09:55 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Galen:<BR><B>Ken, get off your pedastool for a second and read whats been said. You'll see the light(cept in cali). So you are saying these arn't our highways as well? we dont pay taxes on the roads as well? Glad to know you're the only one paying for the roads. Its not fair to force some drivers & passengers to wear seatbelts and others not. my brother had a 57 ford, that didn't have seatbelts, wasn't a option on the car... is it fair to ticket that driver for no seatbelt? when the car didn't come with one?<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well, the laws are written in most areas that you don't have to wear the seat belt, if you're in a car that didn't come with them originally.  We found this out because seat belts were optional in my father's '61 Corvair.  Since they weren't standard equipment, NC law didn't require them to be in the car, and we aren't required to wear them when driving it.  Laws in other states may be different.<P>||||reg||6
Z||000046||Ken Wittlief||06-15-2001||10:24 AM||||Ok, I was posting last night after midnight local time. Now I see what is going on.<P>  you guys that are saying you dont want to be forced to wear your seat belts<P>  you are playing devils advocate on the Forum, so every one else can see how ridiculous it sounds to say "I dont want to wear a seatbelt..." agruing the loosing cause-side of the discussion, to keep the topic going and to get more personal accounts posted of the horrors that happen to people that wont buckle up.<P>   Boy, you really had me going for a while there. Can you just imagine, telling your 16 year old son or duaghter, "now when you ride in a car by yourself, or with your young driver friends, dont let anyone pressure you into WEARING your seatbelt - its written in the US Constitution you know - thats why George Washington and his armys fought off the English, thats why we fought off the Germans, so those domminating control freaks wouldnt be able to stop you from putting your face through this nice tempered glass windsheild.  You make your own choice and dont worry:<P>  If you get into a serious accident, Daddy has a putty knife in his tool box".||||reg||1
Z||000047||Ken Wittlief||06-15-2001||11:36 AM||||BTW, as for getting personal and flaming people - that is my real name and location at the left of my post, if you look me up in the phone book you can find my number and address too.<P>  I believe that using my real idenity on public forums means I am standing behind everthing i say, and that I am who I say I am - ie, im not a 12 year old kid prenteding to be a 43 year old engineer...<P>   In my honest opinion, with all the crash testing that has been done, and with all the autopsies, and fatal accident investigations that have been conducted, with the mountain of expensive data that has been accumulated, if you drive on the road without wearing your seatbelts, you gotta be nuts.<P>   Bonehead is what you will be when the ambulance comes to scrap you off the road - all that will be left of your head is the bone, the contents will be splattered everwhere else.<P>  Organ Donor is also an applicable title.<P>  Is that getting personal? Yes! If someone reads this topic and decides to start wearing their seatbelts, and their life is saved in an upcoming accident, will WE have PERSONALLY been responsible for saving their life?<P>   YES!<P>  another forum I am active on discusses sailing beachcats, hobie cat sailboats and other similar ones.<P>  we had a young guy come on the forum about six months ago, an extreem sports advocate, who sailed in the atlantic ocean on a hobie cat, alone, in cold weather.<P>  Several people on the forum pointed out to him in as friendly a manner as possible, that he was taking a terrible risk being out on the ocean, in cold water, alone, with no radio or means to summon help.<P>  about a month ago, someone posted a the contents of a newpaper report of a sailor who had capsized his boat out on the ocean, and was found about 10 hours later by the Coast Guard, dead from hypothermia.<P> It was him.<P>  Its a horrible thing to go back and look at the messages he posted on the forum, and the responses he received, the advice he was given that WOULD have saved his life if he had TAKEN IT PERSONALLY, instead of generally.<P>  Likewise, this forum is 100% personal. You post messages with a real or psudo screen name, the posts here are not all bunched together in one long steam of anon postings. <P>   Also, the roads are not MY roads. We all throw our money into a big pile, and we vote on what we are gonna to with it, and we come to a general consences on the way the public systems will be used.<P>   cars have been around for 100 years now. They are no longer a buggy with a motor that replaces the horse - they are an integrated transportation sytem that is designed to be used with all occupants STRAPPED into their seats. One out of four drivers WILL be in a serious accident during their lifetime. <P>  Thats simply the way it is.<P>  and thats all I have to say about that.||||reg||1
Z||000048||Butter||06-15-2001||11:45 AM||||I must agree with Songman after reading the thread.  <P>Check out some of my satistics.<BR>1. I have been in the back of a truck that rolled down a 20' bank and come to rest on its side.  I got thrown from the truck and got a scratch on my left little finger.<BR>2. Took a roll in a VW Bus with 2 of my brothers and an axe bouncing around with us in there.  It landed back on its wheels and we drove that beat up thing home so my brother could get his a$$ whipped for showin' out.  My other brother got a small bruise in the accident, the one driving got one on his but from dad and Mom give me a hug.<BR>3. Had a head on with my mother and sister in a 66 Galaxy.  We all got minor bruises and it totaled new the car.<BR>4. Had a head on with the neighbor in my cousin’s car while he was driving.  He busted his lip on the steering wheel.  Damn those narrow gravel roads back then.<BR>5. Was going to visit a friend with him driving us and a truck was backing up in the middle of the road under the crest of the hill, we smacked him in the a$$ end.  Brian hurt his wrist but was ok.  I lost the tape deck I was holding but it survived.<BR>6. Rolled and totaled my 68 Opel doin' the Rockford Files reverse 180 with my neighbor in there with me.  He scratched his hand and never asked me to cut up with him again.<BR>7. Rear ended a lady in Memphis that totaled my brother’s 69 Mustang, hurt my feelings really bad.<BR>8. Sitting still in a parking lot in my buddy’s truck and a car rams my door.  Ricky thought I was getting sweet on him till the impact.  Got a little bruise on my leg.  Don’t know if it was from the impact or Ricky tryin’ to get me away from him. haha<BR>9. Lost control of my Cougar in a curve from a flat I was having.  Banged and bruised my head in that one.  Turned my ankle getting out of the car into the ditch.<P>These are the times I have been in accidents with no seat belts.  I wont count the times I helped the county clean ditches or helped  the TWRA thin out the deer population.<P>1. Rolled my dune buggy two different times on the same hill one day end over end both times.  Scratched my leg and bruised my side pretty good.  Broke the hub on the bug the last time.<BR>2. Run the tire off my Fiero in a curve and run threw the neighbor’s fence, Hurt my feelings really bad again.<BR>The neighbor builds good fences.  It saved me from the trees behind it.<P>These are the times I have been in accidents with seat belts<P>My Conclusion:<BR>The good lord takes care of fools, drunks, and children.  <BR>I’m getting older now and wear my seat belt most of the time but I’m afraid to quit drinking.<P>Yall figure.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000049||flyntus||06-15-2001||12:22 PM||||I automatically put mine on. Sometimes I don't even realize that I did.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000050||DecadenceR||06-15-2001||01:08 PM||||In NJ they can now pull you over for not wearing your seatbelt.<BR>I have been making a conscious effort to wear it, but I still hate them. Before I started driving, I saw a guy that had an accident. he was wearing his belt. because of the way he hit, he snapped his neck. He lived, but will never move again. I don't want to be that guy.||||reg||1
Z||000051||FieroPerformance||06-15-2001||01:15 PM||||I ALWAYS wear my seatbelt. It saved my life when I rolled my parents Blazer over doing about 60. Plus I have slid off the road at least twice in my Fiero.. One time I bounced in the air off of a curb and that probably would have hurt a lot more if I hadn't of been wearing a seatbelt. It did but a slight dent in one of my new rims though  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>-Charlie<BR>-------<BR>jackalrr@hotmail.com||||reg||1
Z||000052||84Bill||06-15-2001||01:27 PM||||   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by LZeitgeist:<BR><B><BR>It seems that the Government's role in today's society is to save society from Darwin's influence. No wonder society is going to Hell in a handbasket - nothing left to cull the weak and the stupid<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Heheh took me 1/2 hour to edit down to this simple statement<BR>But I liked it so much it was worth the effort. Now that I have this information I think I will take a lighter stance next time.<BR>Please feel free not to wear seatbelts    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>I will continue to use them and my children WILL be FORCED to use them until they are old enough to decide for themselves.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 06-15-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000053||elfiero||06-15-2001||01:46 PM||||I'm 44-when I started driving at 15,I didn't<BR>wear a belt.at 16,I started racing and was<BR>required to wear it.I raced until I was 42<BR>and I've run into more sh*t than I can remem-<BR>ber.One time I hit a wall head on over 100mph<BR>and blacked out from deceleration-woulda<BR>been dead without the 5pt harness.I've had<BR>my belts come loose during a race-you go to<BR>the pits immediately because you would'nt<BR>belive how much you move around in a race car<BR>even with a custom fitted seat.I belive that<BR>Darwin was correct,so people should do what<BR>they feel is right...It will just leave that<BR>many more fieros for the rest of us to enjoy<BR>after the genetic pool has been thinned a<BR>little more.P.S.-on the way home from the <BR>races that first night when I was 16,I reached down and hooked up the seatbelt for<BR>the first time and I have used it ever since||||reg||6
Z||000054||LZeitgeist||06-15-2001||02:52 PM||||LZeitgeist wrote:<BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>It seems that the Government's role in today's society is to save society from Darwin's influence. No wonder society is going to Hell in a handbasket - nothing left to cull the weak and the stupid.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>It's more meaningful if you quote the entire statement.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Glad you liked it.||||reg||1
Z||000055||Ken Wittlief||06-15-2001||03:13 PM||||so wearing a seatbelt goes against the laws of nature<P>  wait.. I think..  where is that bright light coming from?<P>  I SEE it NOW! wearing a seatbelt is UN-NATURAL!<P>  you guys have convinced me - Im getting a knife and cutting all the seat belts out of my cars.<P>  I need to come up with a slogan and make my own bunmper stickers. lets see:<P>  Its my face and its my windsheild<P>  Born Free to die Free &lt;- better yet:<P>  I was born without a seatbelt, and by God Ill die without one too!<P>  Rebel without a clasp<P>  If God had wanted me to wear a seatbelt I would have been born with a buckle instead of a navel<P>  Dont tell me what to do with my seatbelt and I wont tell you what to do with your gearshift<P>  Ban seatbelts: Gods will be done - on earth, asphalt leads to heaven.<P>  PARAMETICS: Open me first - organ donors inside<P>  I havent been paying all this life insurance for nothing!<P>  Special offer: stop wearing your seatbelts and get a free ride in a Helecopter!<P>  seatbelts are for babies and women: brace yourself and take it like a man!<P>  If you think my face is ugly, you should see what I did to the tree!<P><p>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 06-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000056||84Bill||06-15-2001||03:25 PM||||Your right LZ  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> I fixed it, looks better in bold   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Ken! simmer down man your gonna suffer an anurism, and then what will I do? I wont be sitting here spending hours reading your complicated responces to basic issues that I've come to enjoy so much  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 06-15-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000057||fierohoho||06-15-2001||04:09 PM||||Two things, a request and a funny story.<P>Request:<P>Ken, don't cut your seatbelts out, go to a t-shirt printing place and get a white shirt printed with a black stripe from the left shoulder to the right hip. At a glance it will look like you have your belt on then get a will drawn up so if you crash and die I get all the rest of your cars with the seatbelts intact for me to use.<P>Story:<P>I get called to a two vehicle collision at a stop light on the highway. A big old 4dr ran a red light and was broadsided on the passenger side by a newer van with airbags.<P>No injuries, lucky, I ask the lady from the van if she was OK, she said she was sore from left shoulder to right hip from seat belt but otherwise OK. <P>I look in the van, airbag deployed, I ask do you remember hitting the airbag and she said no I didn't hit it, I hold the airbag flat to show her she did in fact hit the bag as there was a perfect pair of lipstick prints on the bag, boy did we laugh. <P>Oh, the guy in the 4dr was OK but wondering why we were laughing, he saw nothing funny about the situation. He got a ticket, he didn't think that was funny either.<P>------------------<BR>'84 SE 2.5l<BR>'85 Coupe 2.5l now getting a 3800<BR>'86 SE 2.5l||||reg||1
Z||000058||JSocha||06-15-2001||04:57 PM||||Well, as you have probably not noticed, I'm staying out of this thread.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>DAMN! I did it again!  Wanted to keep my nose out of it and here I am!<P>Well, since I am here, BUTTER, first things first, I would never go driving around with you with or without a seatbelt when your present.  Accidents seem to follow yah! j/k  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Somebody mentioned wearing seatbelts on an airplane.  Only on take off and landing (or during turbulant weather).  Once off the ground, you are free to remove them if you wish, even in private planes.  Not allowed to do so in cars where laws are enacted for the public's safety.<P>Next, if we should follow the rest of society and society feels we should be protected, then I guess we should all get on the bandwagon and enact laws against the use of motorcycles, especially the ones high performance with jet rockets for motors.  At 140+ MPH, you hit something, your dead (why are the seatbelts not required there? that's a rhetorical question BTW) or have a hellish road rash and on a very fast weight loss program because half ones @ss is going to be smeared where a putty knife will be required.<P>We talk about society, laws from our own state goverenments up to the federal laws that are passed for our protection and we should abide by them.  Then why don't our politicians following these same laws we must all abide by.<P>Law in ND, no tinted windows immidately up front on the drivers and/or passangers side.  Behind you can paint your windows black if you wish.  Go down to the Capital of Bismarck where the law was submitted and passed, goverenment vehicles and privately owned, tinted windows all the way to the front with 5% or less (darkest tint you can get).<P>ND has a seatbelt law, however, you can only be sited if your are pulled over for another offence and found not to be wearing them.  Again, law passed by our state supported and passed by our officials, yet, since Senator Solberg is from Rugby and very close friends with our family, I learned that a majority of our state officials don't wear their seat belts, but we damn wells better.  And public school buses for our kids don't have them installed and are not required to as Hartley's School Buses here in town has also confirmed as our familys are good friends (small community...Peyton place).<P>The Transportation boards within your state as well as the National Transportation board, yes they do their jobs researching to keep the dangers of driving and other methods of transportation down.  Yet, on the other side, these institutions were also developed to be "money" generating organizations for the goverenment to fund the goverenment, not just for repairing our roads, traffic research, etc., but to line the buecrats pockets as well.<P>Many states now have a "maximum" speed limit to which they can set to which all cars are allowed to drive, lets say for the sake of argument, that the maximum is 75 MPH across the board, after that, you would be obviously cited for speeding is that correct?  Well, this wasn't the case a few years ago when there was a "FEDERAL" limit.  So then why (didn't we) not govern them all so they can never go above (55MPH) 75 MPH, then there would be no deaths due to people exceeding the imposed speed limit maximum.  But of course, Uncle Sam wouldn't be able to collect any money.<P>We all make choices in life, for good and for bad personally.  Others are imposed on us by laws, but may not suit our individual needs to which we feel comfortable, like the person who says it can't be done, yet the next individual steps up and actually does it, because it was not comfortable for him not to be able to do it.<P>Some talked about that there are higher numbers/statistic for those that survived while wearing their seatbelts as opposed to those that did not.<P>Well, look at these statistics from another angle/perspective.  How many women in our society do not report the physical act of rape, when they should have?  <P>On the other hand, how many women have reported being raped, yet the figures were not reported in order to either keep the crime statitics against women is going down in order to look good nationally as a whole?<P>Same thing could be done here with the use of seatbelts versus that against it and the numbers reported.  Maybe there are more survivors out there then we know of that did not wear their seatbelts and survived, versus those that did and died and were not reported to make the numbers look good.<P>Just like how many of us have jumped to be recognized for something great?  How about something that one would be embarrased about?<P>Numbers can be skewed and manipulated to get the desired result and/or for support on a debate.<P>To those of you who do wear your seatbelt (as does my wife)...that is great and is your personal choice to do so.  <P>I do not think of you any less for doing so and would appreciate the same consideration for my choice of not wearing one, as I do not.  I use to, but I do not anymore for reasons explained on another similar thread about a month or so ago.<P>Even though I was baptized Roman Catholic as a child, I do not believe in a Heaven and/or a Hell and therefore I do not believe in a superior power and/or a God.  <P>Others do (and I know I'm going to get flamed for this, possibly from one particular member...remeber...I'm talking religion here...not drugs!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ).  <P>They are entitled to their rights and beliefs regarding religion without me imposing my beliefs on them and to which I do not judge them for what they do and/or what they believe in regarding God.  And I appreciate when people respect me and my beliefs and/or disbeliefs regarding religion.<P>Now, from time to time, I do get strong suited, especially on the "420" thread before I had to sit back and tell myself, wait a minute Jeff...why am I now trying to impose my belief and/or trying to change their views regarding their choices in life. Humand nature, I guess. I do not know if that would constitute an apology, yet, in that way it was.<P>Sorry if this has gotten OT here in GFC.<P>Peace to you who choose to wear yours and Peace to those that choose not to!<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000059||Voytek||06-15-2001||06:01 PM||||Seatbelts all the way.  Here's another story: one of my best friends had a Fiero.  In '93 he was 19 and only had his license for 3 months and his fiero for 1 month.  To make a long story short, after a short race between us, he rear-ended a Greyhound bus at about 70-80 km/h (45-50mph) that was pulling out of a gas station (into the back SIDE).  I was sure he was either badly hurt or dead.  The car was a twist, fluids all over the road, driver's door jammed, sunroof and steering wheel all crooked.  The bus? It was severely tilted to one side and they had to move the passengers to a different one.  My friend?  A sore chest from the seatbelt and no other injuries.  On a side note, I have never seen anyone get out of a Fiero this fast through the passenger side.<BR>Anyways, Cliff - I couldn't agree more - for every story of 'BAD' seatbelts, there are a thousand stories about seatbelts saving lives.<BR>SongMan- I'm not intending this to sound like flaming, but you say you may only have used up 1% of all the money you've paid into the system over the years.  What if you were to be paralysed in an accident for the rest of your life and unable to work?  Do you know how fast the system/insurance would run out of YOUR contributions by caring for you?  People always seem to think 'if I don't wear a seatbelt and die, it's my choice'.  You never hear anyone say 'if I don't wear a seatbelt and become a vegetable, and have to eat a Big Mac through a straw, piss into a bottle and can't even wipe my as$, it's my choice'.  And forget the money and physical things for a minute, don't you care about the emotional pain of your close ones?  I have to agree with you on the Gore thing, though.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>To all those of you who think you're oppressed by the government: sometimes people are too stupid to think for themselves, that's why we have elected officials.  If you don't like their decisions, vote for someone else next time.<BR>KW: I love the bumper stickers - very true.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000060||Voytek||06-15-2001||06:18 PM||||JSocha - you must've been writing while I was writing (off and on for an hour).  I'll stay out of the religion thing, but Rape and Seatbelts????  Yeah, I'm sure those of us who were not wearing seatbelts and got in an accident don't report it because we're too embarrassed and are afraid no one will believe us.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">  And as far as numbers' manipulation:  Think about it.  The government USUALLY likes to spend as little money as possible on us.  The same goes for the Insurance companies.  So what's the conclusion?  Chances are that these institutions are advocating wearing seatbelts BECAUSE they have to spend LESS money on us, i.e. less injuries WITH seatbelts.  Common sense, man.  No flame intended.||||reg||1
Z||000061||Songman||06-15-2001||07:41 PM||||Jsocha, this is intended for you since I am not even gonna get involved in this thread again...<P>The reason I am not is the same reason I am now suggesting you don't. These people aren't even reading what we are saying. They seem to think that we are saying that we knoe we can't get hurt so we don't need seatbelts. We both know that you can get hurt (in certain kinds of accidents) if you don't wear a seatbelt. What they don't read in our words is that it is still our choice. No matter how many horror stories are printed here, that still doesn't give anyone the right to legislate choice. And it sure doesn't give people the right to think that just because they do it that we should...<P>I tried to stay out of it before because of the very same reason. Our point is entirely lost on... well, the people who think like I listed above. I'm not gonna go into it anymore... And I'm still above calling people names like so many on the other side of this issue can't help doing... Guess I'm just stupid! hehe<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000062||JSocha||06-15-2001||08:25 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Voytek:<BR><B>To all those of you who think you're oppressed by the government: sometimes people are too stupid to think for themselves, that's why we have elected officials.  If you don't like their decisions, vote for someone else next time.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Nah!  We just pick the best liars for our "elected" officials.  And who says they are any smarter.<P>Oh!  And no flame taken...I just move on and go on with my life...<P>Songman, Agreed!  I'm out of here as well since you already understand my position on this from our previous posts and without sounding like a hypocrit (as I know I'm going to get burned someplace for something else I have said before I realized what I was doing) to the rest of you, all I ask is don't cram your specific beliefs down others throats.<P>To the rest of you, gone...no...another thought...what do any of you really care what I do, cause you don't know me from Adam, just like I don't know you personally, so what does it really matter to you whether or not I believe in wearing or not wearing seatbelts?  Its not your life to play with...but mine and mine alone to play Russian Roulette with as I see fit.  <P>If I want to live on the edge, I will live on the edge.  If you don't, then don't.<P>Nuff said...Sayanara tomadachi!<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000063||84Bill||06-15-2001||08:40 PM||||<B>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JSocha:<P>I have said before I realized what I was doing) to the rest of you, all I ask is don't cram your specific beliefs down others throats.<P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Umm it's the law not a belief, since I'm a newly converted believer in the Darwin theory, go and do as you wish and hell with the law street race, heck street race on curved roads and don't wear a seatbelt   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <P>Thanks again LZ, that was one cool epiphany<BR><p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 06-15-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000064||LZeitgeist||06-15-2001||08:50 PM||||Ken & Bill - go back and read my original post.<P>I wear my seatbelt because I feel it is the safer choice for me to make. <P>However, I don't need the government to force me to do what I feel is in my best interest.<P>I don't wear it because the government tells me to - I wore my seat belt religiously for 5 years before NC ever *thought* of making it into law. Because I feel its the wiser choice for me.<P>I don't care if I'm the only human being on Earth who wears a seatbelt - make your own choice. But if you live, let it be because you were smart enough to survive - not because the government saved you from your own stupidity.<P>It's that caveat right there that I rail against with the quoted statement up there - because of the government protecting stupid people from themselves, those of us who have the common sense to survive are forced to live in the same society as those with no common sense whatsoever.<P>I can't tell if I'm being taken out of context in jest, or out of misunderstanding. However, I prefer not to be quoted out of context. Twisting my words removes them from my realm of responsibility, and puts them into yours.<P>Anyway, who gives a flip - it's Friday... the weekend has arrived.<p>[This message has been edited by LZeitgeist (edited 06-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000065||84Bill||06-15-2001||09:22 PM||||LZ <BR>I too have worn a seatbelt for 90% of my driving life and even before that since my father was a pilot. I was just prompted by someone's comment on "ramming beliefs" down someone's throat, hence "it's the law" not a belief that some uphold here. I happen to not care for cretin laws but I live by most like required drivers licenses (totally useless), auto insurance (legal shammers in a self perpetuated money hungry system) and a krap load of others. Trust me I read your post several times. The law statement was just a low stab to elicit a responce and if he responds before he gets to this post I'll have readings to snicker at. <BR>It's Friday and I safely made it home where I intend to stay, nursing my beer and reading posts until Saturday morning when it's safer to go back outside and drive on the roads again.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000066||Ken Wittlief||06-15-2001||11:14 PM||||Im sitting here reading over the posts again and another incredibly important fact comes to mind.<P>  You are out driving around in your car without your seatbelt. Someone, lets say my daughter, is driving along in her car, and makes a minor mistake, reaches down to change the radio station, doesnt see you pull out, she hits you head on, you go through the windshield and you are dead.<P> Since she had her seatbelt on she has only minor injuries. If you had your seatbelt on you would have minor injuries too.<P>  But since you were exercizing your freedom of choice that 'affects nobody but you', now you are dead.<P>  the police come, place the blame on my daughter for YOUR DEATH, and now at best I and my insurace company get sued for tens of millions of dollars, I loose my house, she can no longer go to college.<P>  at worst she is charged with MANSLAUGHTER and goes to prison for 5 years.<P>  In realitiy who is responsible for your death? - you are: because you had the safety equipment at your disposal and refused to use it. <P>  Who will be held accountable for your death, My daughter will. I will. My insurance company will. The company that made your car will be sued.<P>  Thats why its a law. How can you possible say what you do only affects you?!?!<P>  How can you say your death wont destroy the lives of the other people that are involved in the accident?<P>  How do you know you wont be knocked unconsciouse, or thrown into the back seat, while your now, out of control car, plows into a group of school children?<P>  How do you know the first person that arrives on the scene of your death wont have a nervious breakdown, or be tramatized for life?<P>   I swear to God - I simply cannot believe ANYONE could watch the slow motion vidoes of crash tests and watch the dummies in the one car strain against their seat belts and sustain little impact, and then watch the dummies in the second car, with the front seat passengers hitting the windshield and their necks bending back till their heads hit their spine, and the kids in the back seat going through the windshield<P>  and saying, "Thats what I want! I wanna be in the second car! No seat belts for me!<P>  I give up - I really do. This is depressing. After this 'open exchange of thoughts and ideas' on a subject that should be a no-brainer for the last two days, I now have a seriously different opinion of the average intelligence of the people out driving on the roads and highways. <P>   This is downright frightening - it really is.<P>    and extreemly sad.<P>   If EVERYBODY that drives cant look at the facts concerning this simple issue and all come to the same conclusion, then I hate to even think what is going on in peoples minds concerning more complex things. <P>  Living on an island somewhere is starting to sound like a good idea.||||reg||11
Z||000067||Voytek2||06-15-2001||11:36 PM||||Songman and JSocha: I think we hear you loud and clear: freedom of choice.  Unfortunately, in the name of freedom, people have done stupider things than not wearing their seatbelts.  It's like a little kid saying 'I would rather freeze my ears off than wear the took that my mom bought', when it's 30 below. Well, his ears may or may not fall off, but that's the chance he's taking, right?  No one is saying that you're not entitled to your opinion (which you have stated with a passion).  Ken has got some excellent points and it would REALLY be hard to ignore them and not take them into consideration.  While a few people have responded to YOUR arguments, you have not made any comments about ours.  The main thing you have to say is 'Nobody is going to tell me what to do!'. Is that what this is about??!! Some kind of silly pride??  And I DON't even want to get into the IQ thing, Songman.||||reg||1
Z||000068||JSocha||06-16-2001||01:25 AM||||We can all sit here and play "what if" all night, year, decads or eons long.  No matter how or what you gamble on, its a 50/50 chance.  Any gambler will tell you that. Some odds yes will be better, some won't.  Depends on the circumstances and severity of the incident.<P>I have friends on the EMT, not only here in Rugby, but around the state and abroad.  (paraphrasing here) In talking with them over the years, many say they have in their life time, packaged many people up in body bags for the "meat wagon" from the serious to the not so serious vehicle accidents while at the same time, where many of these now dead individuals were in fact "wearing their seatbelts" and had to be cut out of them.  Didn't help them.  And yes, others were not many died, many survived.  I'm not debating that issue.<P>The issue again is "personal choice".<P>As far as my use of the term "beliefs" versus "law" in my post, well I don't mind if you got hung up on the symantics of the terminology from the phrasing.  That wasn't my intention or point.  <P>I just don't need to be called "stupid" just because I as an indivdual do and choose not to wear them any more then I would call you "overly intelligent" and worship the ground you walk on for your choice in wearing them.<P>You "believe" in them, great.  I as well as other do not or may not "believe" in them, and all we ask that you leave us alone on those beliefs, regardless if it is a law or not.  <P>Yet suddenly I as well as others are immidately labeled a stupid SOB for not using them.  You got your facts and arguments to support your position, as do I for my position on the matter.<P>However, I'm also not here telling you to "NOT WEAR THEM", and as clearly stated by another individual, am I?  <P>As I believe (yep theres that word again in a different contextual form) the topic of this thread was "who wears their seatbelts".  <P>I finally responded and threw in my measly 2 cents (or maybe I should have thrown more into this kitty to make many others happy because of the "cost" involved) along with a few others, and the guns come out blasting with basically "you better understand this or that" and "we all pay for your choice".  <P>We all pay irregardless, either way!  For example, our insurance premiums we pay are based on a national average which can go up as a result of those people that like to speed no matter how many times they get caught.  <P>Premiums will be raised on the national level as well as on our individual policy's based on ours or others driving records in the long run, irregardless.<P>Well, if thats the case, I don't want to be paying for higher premiums because some of you may decide to go 20+ MPH over the posted speed limit whereas I tend to drive the limit and haven't sped in over a 1 1/2 years now, because I'm working on getting my premium down on the personal level.<P>Therefore, I'm now in Washington DC to lobby the goverenment to get back on the wagon and regulate all states again to the 55MPH Maxium posted speed limit across the board.  And in order to uphold this and save taxpayers money, I'm going to make sure that they regulate all imports, domestic and all vehicles currently on the road today to be goverend so they can not exceed 55 MPH.<P>Pretty soon, and probably many forum members as well proud of their modified Fieros, ricers, muscle cars, etc., will be stating that their rights to put in a super-charged, turbo and or jet motor for the purpose of going faster and having the fastest vehicle on the block, as their choice, is now being constitutionally violated.<P>But you know something, how many of you, so we don't have to pay for it, are on a bandwagon to do what I just had proposed here?<P>And you know what...we'll all be here arguing about that too, who does and doesn't have the rights of "free choice" and that we pay for that too.<P>If I can recall upon a previous post for a moment, I will call upon that individuals general statement, ie, regarding basically we elect officials because some of us our too stupid to know the difference.<P>Well if that's the case as this would be easier to enforce nationally then that of a seatbelt law and if it was based on the aforementioned to impose such a goverened maxium speed that the car can not go beyond mechanically, many of you with the technical know-how will be out there tinkering to get around that "damn govenor" so you can go faster then the rest, because you will feel it is "your choice".<P>And I will go from the standpoint of human nature.  Many of you might be on the supporting side of the seatbelts. Fine! Fine...no problem there.  <P>But now will throw the shoe on to the other foot where the fit will not be as good and just say they are hitting that sensative nerve on you regarding how fast you can go versus how fast you believe you should be able to go.  You'll soon be off this topic and supporting your choice for the right to go faster!<P>Then when people start to jump your $h*t, I guarantee you your going to be defensive to your position/stand on the now "hot" issue.<P>Yes FUNGUY, I may be or sound like a hypocrit from your post to this.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by JSocha (edited 06-16-2001).]||||reg||8
Z||000069||JSocha||06-16-2001||02:35 AM||||Me again!<P>Just to throw another fanatical spin on this topic which came to mind.<P>How many of you out there that are supporting "the use of seatbelts", either taking a direct and active part in this thread or sitting on the sidelines cheering the supporters of the seatbelts with the basic thought "thats right...you tell em man!" are also:<P>1) of legal age, smoking and having more then the recommended glasses of wine and/or beer as recommended by health authorties.<BR>2) under age and smoking, drinking, taking any illegal drugs, etc. yada-yada-yada<BR>3) taking illegal drugs regularily<BR>4) have a drinking problem (ie, becoming or getting a light/heavy buzz)<BR>5) driving under the influence and/or after 1 to several drinks.<BR>6) initiating in unprotected sex (with someone you are currently seeing and/or with multiple partners...you get the drift)<BR>7) overweight for your height and/or to which you do not exercise regualarly<P>Well, if your supporting the use of seat belts because it costs society in general and you are doing one or any or more of the above mentioned, you in turn are raising the medical premiums I end up paying on my health insurance policy for myself and my family.<P>I do not smoke<BR>I do not take drugs<BR>I have a drink or two occassionaly if the wife or I go out for supper together and/or with friends<BR>I do have sex, only with my wife and when she is in the mood...and with that statement I end up safer then most of you I would say because it is so sacred in our house, I have to pray for it all the time...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>I'm exercising now and working on loosing weight<BR>etc....<P>So please quit your bad habits because it is costing me as well as society in whole and in general!<P>Oh????  I see...Yeah! Yeah! Oh? I understand...its your "personal" choice...<P>Oh!? I have to live with it you say?!<P>OH! Due to the fact its your body, not mine?  <P>Yeah! I can see that &lt;as I'm waiving away second had smoke from those that feel they have the right to smoke in my face and while in mine or others presence&gt;.<P>Would you or do you feel a little like a hypocrit as well now?<P>BTW, I have nothing against you all, as I consider everyone here on the forum not only a friend (as tomodachi is Japanese for "friend") and as family members.<P>I take some advise here that I am comfortable with and which will fit and will adapt it to my lifestyle.  And some advise I have to throw to the side as I am not or do not feel comfortable with it.<P>Besides, I think I might be all riled up cause my blood sugar levels must be through the roof again if I go by what the doctors have been tellin me lately!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  And yes! Irritability I found is a symptom of a problem I am having.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
