Z||000098||Toddster||06-17-2001||10:51 PM||||97 posts! and I thought the "Apology" thread was getting out of hand.<P>OK, here is a seatbelt story for you. I broke my collar bone in a biking accident years ago. The bone never fully healed and it gives me the heebee jeebees now and then (that's the medical term for arthritis). I got a note from my doctor so that I would not haveto wear my seatbelt if it bothered me.<P>I try to wear it but sometimes I am in a hurry and forget. Anyway, if I do forget and get stopped by a cop, I have the doctor's note nice and handy behind my driver license. Haven't been cited since.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000099||Songman||06-17-2001||11:14 PM||||Dang! I broke my collar bone in a biking accident way back in 1976. I wonder if I can still get a doctor's note on it?!? hehe<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||12
Z||000100||filthyscarecrow||06-18-2001||12:53 AM||||i think that people who don't wear seatbelts should have to waive their right to sue for injuries when they get in an accident, even if it's not their fault. they could have prevented the injuries, they chose not to, therefore, why should the other party have to pay? you might say that they caused an accident. the problem with that lies in the fact that any reasonable human being, upon slithering into the cockpit, assumes a certain risk that he/she will get involved in an accident. this is something that, unless you are of monumental stupidity, you know is a possibility. which leads to the argument for wearing seatbelts. if you know that there is a possibility of you getting into an accident, and you know that wearing a seatbelt is more likely to help you than hurt you, why WOULDN'T you put one on? it's not necessarily stupidity so much as it is ignorance. you see the risks, but you ignore them. hopefully it also means that you're an organ donor. tell me, do you also play a lot of russian roullette? <P>------------------<BR>allow me to show you my exhaust pipes...||||reg||1
Z||000101||Galen||06-18-2001||01:18 AM||||ummm.. ok<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/pictures/redcreature.gif">     <BR> <A HREF="mailto:galen@spinrpg.com">galen@spinrpg.com</A>  -<A HREF="http://myfiero.spinrpg.com/" TARGET=_blank>1988 Hawaiin Orchid Coupe w/ sunroof</A><BR>AIM: GalenRPG - <A HREF="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/" TARGET=_blank>WebJolt Pictures</A>||||reg||1
Z||000102||JSocha||06-18-2001||01:52 AM||||Just so everyone is clear on one point so it is not misunderstood, I never said seatbelts "don't" work, I just choose not to use them "anymore" or wear them.  <P>Yes...I said "anymore".  <P>I use to wear them religiously all the time, then I rolled my 88 LeBaron at 35 MPH (one roll) on a gravel road with my sister in law.  I ended up outside the vehicle, my sister in law inside still strapped in...and YES I was wearing my seatbelt at the time and still ended up outside the vehicle.<P>The HP officer that arrived at the scene, said quote <I>"Good thing you weren't wearing your seatbelt or you would have been crushed under the vehicle instead of thrown out like you were."</I> unquote.  Those words still will ring in my ears forever.<P>The officer was in disbelief when I had told him that I had been wearing my seatbelt and the seatbelt was still latched when he inspected the vehicle.<P>Sister in law who was also wearing her seatbelt (to which it would not come undone and I ended up having her pull her lap strap loose after we slipped the shoulder strap over her head so I could lift her out the now broken passenger window because there were sparks flying from under the hood and fuel on the ground so she was out just in case the car exploded) ended up with four times as many injuries then what I sustained, including wiplash to which she was hospitalized for about a month for her injuries!<P>I ran our brand new 1997 Dodge 1-ton pickup into the back tire of a tractor on our way home for lunch one afternoon who decided not to check behind him for vehicles and moved into the opposing lane and now our passing lane over 200+ feet before his driveway while we were in the process of passing him.<P>Now I had been wearing my seatbelt off and on since we got our new truck.  On this particular day, I didn't put it on but my wife had put hers on.  We rearended the tractor tire smack dab in the center of my hood (air bags did not deploy) and caused over 15K worth of damage just to the front end of our truck and no damage to the tractor.  <P>I walked away without any scratchs, bruises or injuries.  I was just shaken up a bit and upset that our new truck was now damaged.  My wife on the other hand, with a seatbelt on, ended up in the hospital with injuries, a scratch and quite a few bruises and had to go for checkups once a week.<P>Before I met my wife, as she as well as my oldest step-daughter has relayed this story many times to me, had gotten T-Boned in Bismarck when she was living there with her previous husband and was wearing her seatbelt and ended up in the hospital for over a month and in rehabilitation for several months afterwards.  She still suffers from pain to this day as a result of that accident including that she can not remember things as well as she once did and is a little slower, both physically and mentally.<P>The officer at her accident basically told her, her family and her friends at the hospital, if she would "not" have been wearing her seatbelt, she would have had "less" injuries then what she sustained.  <P>But because she "did" wear her seatbelt, she ended up with a lot more injuries and ended up in the hospital on top of it.<P>This was the reason that was given to them by the officer:  Seatbelts only work in frontal and/or rear end collisions and are less effective when it is a side impact.<P>Well, if I'm wearing my seatbelt, I see a car heading towards my drivers side and it is inevitable we are going to connect, it will be good by Charlie and/or a lot of injuries for me.<P>But if I'm not wearing my seatbelt, at least I can push my fat @ss towards the passenger side, grab on to something and let where I was sitting buckle in and not pinch me to boot.  Vise versa for my passengers (see photos via the link on the PLEASE be serious thread HEADING: T-Boned.<P>Again, to reiterate to the masses, "I never said seatbelts DO NOT work as I know they do and are there for that specific purpose".  <P>I have just chosen over time not to, and I respectfully do not need you, my neighbors, my family (both biological and current family) and especially the goverenment telling me that I have to for this reason or that.<P>I herewith, on this 18th day of June, 2001 relieve you of that responsibility, duty and/or fire you if that is necessary from this respective job that I never hired you for in the firstplace as you are not on my payroll.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>I am very glad to those of you who DO wear your seatbelts and I also wish that there never comes a time where they have to actually be put to the test in an accident.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000103||84Bill||06-18-2001||06:29 AM||||Well that just goes to show the even a blind hog can find an acorn. <BR>I don't think anyone here is going to say seatbelts are the be all end all of car safety. Just that in "frontal" impacts your less open to injury and death. With that I can safely say the I should ride in an SUV without a seatbelt and keep the windows rolled down due to the high probability the the SUV I'm riding in will roll over in an accident, that way I can be thrown free and have a better chance to survive?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||6
