Z||000110||Ken Wittlief||06-18-2001||02:56 PM||||okay what a cheap way to suck the engineer back into the discussion, dangle some numbers and equations in front of him (c%<P>Velocity  = acceleration * Time<P> and<P>Distance = 1/2 * acceleration * Time^2<P>so 30mph is the same as falling 30 ft<P>55mph, 101 feet<P>70mph, 164 feet<P>  if you want to think survivability, an office building story is 10 feet, so <P> 30 mph is like falling 3 stories<BR> 55 mph is like falling 10 stories<BR> 70 mph is like falling 16 stories<P>  I dont think any of these are considered surviable without a seat belt. If you want to try it, cut the dash board and windsheild out of the car of your choice, lay it on the sidewalk with the part you would hit in a wreck facing up, then leap from the window of your choice into the dashboard/windsheild.<P>  the other side of the equation, deceleration with the seat belt on, is what happens while the front of your car is collapsing, and the seatbelt is decelerating you from the speed you were traveling, to zero.<P>  if the car collapses 2 ft in a 30mph wreck, you pull 15 Gs with your seatbelt on<P>  if it collapse 4ft at 55mph, you pull 25 Gs with you seatbelt<P>  if it collapse 6 feet at 70mph, you pull 27 Gs with your seatbelt on.<P>  If you dont have your seatbelt on, instead of going from 30 to zero in a distance of 2 feet (while the front of your car is crunching up) your car stops and you keep moving forward at 30mph till you hit something.<P>  then you go from 30 to 0 in the distance it takes for that thing to collapse. If its the dashboard, you decelerate in about 2 inches, and pull 182 Gs (instead of 15).<P>  if you go through the windshield it wont hardly slow you at all, then you decelerate when you hit something more solid, tree, concrete...<P>  if the other thing doenst collapse at all, then all your velocity is lost as your skull, chest, or outstreached arms collapse.<P>   15Gs is enough to make you black out for a few seconds (the impact only lasts 1/10th of a second BTW), but its not enough to cause injury. 25Gs will cause injuries to your shoulders, ribs, and hips, but its survivable - esp compaired to dropping 10 stories into the dashboard of your car.<P>  To look at it the otherway, hitting a brick wall at 30mph WITH your seatbelts on is like being dropped into your dashboard<P>  from a distance of 1 foot.<P>  1 foot or 3 stories - its your free choice.||||reg||1
Z||000111||84Bill||06-18-2001||02:57 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Galen:<BR><B>All I know 55-0 in 15-20 feet hurts like hell, with or without a seatbelt.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I can attest to that!<BR>||||reg||14
Z||000112||firstfiero||06-18-2001||03:02 PM||||       Kinda funny this came up because I just started wearing mine. I suddenly don't feel as indestructable at 30 as I did at 20...go figure   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <BR>       Anyway I do agree their shouldn't be a law to force you to wear one. Were all adults here. I don't need the goverment telling me how to live my life in any shape or form! Same with helmet laws...It's your life do want you want as long as you don't hurt others. I know whenever the fight the helmet laws the agree to a law wearing goggels while the ride. It makes sense...You don't wear eye protection something flys in your eye and can CAUSE you to wreck you bike and possibly injure someone else. I've never heard of single accident CAUSED by someone not wearing a helmet or seatbelt. <BR>   <P>------------------<BR>Hey Mr. Ricer come over here and tell me how your stickers make you faster.....<BR>         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/headshot.gif">         <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/firstfiero.gif"> ||||reg||1
